from pathlib import Path
import json,re,hashlib,difflib
R=Path(__file__).parent
notes={
'Amoeba':('FAQ pp10,14','Move all chosen ships simultaneously: you may ooze all twenty before losing your power for stripped home colonies. An ally that uses Amoeba Super to withdraw all ships still counts as having allied. Amoeba Wild can increase a defense ship count of zero.'),
'Anti-Matter':('FAQ p10','An opponent flare prevented by Anti-Matter remains in hand, does not spend that player\'s flare limit, but cannot be attempted again that encounter. Anti-Matter Super must be played anew in each encounter in which its effect is wanted.'),
'Chosen':('FAQ pp7,11','You can be Cosmic Zapped after the cards are drawn and your intended choice is known, within the immediate same-phase zap window. Chosen Super is played after inspecting the drawn cards.'),
'Citadel':('FAQ p11; rulebook p12','Placing citadels does not require a power use; activating them does. Citadels remain when your sheet is turned facedown, but are discarded if your sheet is discarded.'),
'Clone':('FAQ p12','After Sorcerer switches cards, keep the card that ends in front of you. Clone takes precedence over Filch and Fido: a kept card is not discarded. Filch versus Fido follows ordinary timing. Clone Super may choose any compensation amount from the normal amount through double it, subject to the available hand.'),
'Cudgel':('FAQ pp3–4,12','A deal does not trigger this win-only power. Revealing negotiate and later converting it to attack does not count as having revealed attack. Play Cudgel Super anew in each encounter where its effect is wanted.'),
'Fido':('FAQ p13','Take only an actual encounter card played as such, not an encounter card merely discarded by Plague, Fodder or Reserve. Clone keeps before discarding; Fido competes with Filch using ordinary timing.'),
'Filch':('FAQ pp12–13','After Sorcerer switches cards, take the card ending before your opponent. Clone keeping has precedence; Filch versus Fido uses ordinary timing. You cannot take Gambler\'s unchallenged facedown card because it goes beneath the deck rather than to discard. The classic Filch flare is revealed only when its cheating user is caught; it may then be zapped. Its printed Wild permits illicit warp retrieval to colonies or taking deck/discard cards; being caught costs one ship to warp and returns the items caught being filched. Once revealed, deck and discards are placed next to that player. Use only one Filch flare version.'),
'Fodder':('FAQ pp5,13','Choose all cards for a power use at once, not in repeated uses after seeing results. Fodder Super permits attack cards below the opponent\'s value regardless of your own value; it does not permit cards above the opponent\'s. Fodder Wild can join only a side that originally invited you that encounter.'),
'Gambler':('FAQ pp3,14','Against Oracle you must reveal or announce your bluff before Oracle chooses a card. On Gambler Super, the old term token means ship. The standard printed Sorcerer incompatibility is retained here; the FAQ permits relaxing it if players accept that Sorcerer may know the card.'),
'Grudge':('FAQ p14','An ally that initially committed at least one ship to you avoids a grudge token even if those ships later leave, including through Amoeba Super or Force Field. If a token requires non-encounter losses and no eligible ships remain outside the encounter, lose none.'),
'Hacker':('FAQ pp3,15','Compensation versus defender rewards follows normal offense/defense/other-player timing. The selected target cannot interrupt your hand-targeting effect by dumping reinforcements after their Reveal window has passed.'),
'Hate':('FAQ p15','A required new starting hand is drawn before Hate. You may discard Morph: others need Morph or must lose ships, not substitute the card Morph might copy. Against an attack, a lower attack cannot be discarded; if a player declines or lacks a qualifying card, they discard nothing and lose three ships. A Zap after you select a discard makes you keep it. The printed sheet allows equal-or-higher attacks; the FAQ example uses the word higher rather than equal-or-higher. No supplied erratum expressly changes the printed equality threshold.'),
'Healer':('FAQ pp3,15,22','Ships reach warp before being healed, allowing compensation and Remora\'s retrieval trigger. You cannot heal Zombie ships prevented from arriving. Vacuum triggers before healing, and you may then heal Vacuum and its other affected players separately, but never yourself.'),
'Human':('FAQ pp15,18','The zap win works even if one or both revealed negotiate. An opponent revealing negotiate collects compensation only if Human actually revealed attack and their ships reach warp. Against Pacifist, the first win effect under normal timing wins; Loser\'s expressly later upset still reverses the result. Human Super can add eight instead of four and then optionally be discarded to zap the power, or be discarded solely to zap without adding eight.'),
'Kamikaze':('FAQ p16','Choose and sacrifice all ships for a use together before drawing, rather than one at a time while inspecting draws. Play Kamikaze Super anew each encounter in which its effect is wanted.'),
'Machine':('FAQ pp16–17','Machine Wild must be played when wanted: it guarantees a second encounter and permits a new hand if needed. Play the flare faceup, discard the old hand and draw a new one when needed, then return the flare to hand after its effect finishes. Machine Super also must be played anew each encounter.'),
'Macron':('FAQ pp6,17','A ship-count effect counts each physical Macron ship as one, not four; its power\'s explicit compensation/rewards multiplier remains two. FAQ permits zapping on launch to allow up to four ordinary ships, or after reveal to remove the four-per-ship value. Macron Super\'s up-to-four allowance includes defensive allies. These FAQ zap windows supplement the original Launch/Alliance timing strip.'),
'Masochist':('FAQ p17','Play Masochist Super anew in each encounter where its effect is wanted.'),
'Mind':('FAQ pp17–18','Miser\'s hoard is not a hand you can inspect. Mind Wild must be played anew each encounter where wanted, then lasts through that encounter.'),
'Miser':('FAQ pp17–18','Cards that return to hand, including flares, return to the same hand/hoard from which played. A required hand discard, including Hate, may be paid from hoard. An encounter card only in your hoard does not authorize a second encounter: you need one in your primary hand. Play Miser Super anew each encounter; Miser Wild may save fewer than two cards.'),
'Observer':('FAQ p18','Protection applies during Resolution only. As ally it protects all your own ships, including outside the encounter; as main player it protects all allies\' ships, including remote losses. Grudge can thus relocate protected ships instead of losing them, and a qualifying Void Wild sacrifice can be prevented. It does not protect you merely because you are a main player, nor protect against Void eradication.'),
'Oracle':('Rulebook p14; FAQ p18','Against Sorcerer, foresee first: Sorcerer plays faceup, then Oracle chooses facedown; after both are selected Sorcerer can switch them. Against Gambler, Gambler must reveal or bluff before Oracle chooses.'),
'Pacifist':('FAQ pp15,18','Against zapped Human, the first conflicting win effect in normal timing order wins. If Loser calls an upset while you must reveal negotiate, you receive compensation only if Loser revealed attack and your ships actually reach warp.'),
'Philanthropist':('FAQ pp6,18','The given card can be seen before the immediate Cosmic Zap decision. With Philanthropist Super, defense must play the given card and cannot invoke general timing to avoid it. A power loan using Philanthropist Wild includes associated holdings such as Miser\'s hoard.'),
'Reincarnator':('FAQ p19','The Reincarnator flare\'s corrected timing is after the end of the encounter, so it does not stop a same-phase power use during the encounter. Its Wild targets another player, never yourself. You cannot use the Super flares of your incarnations.'),
'Remora':('FAQ pp15,19','Healer returning actual warp losses can trigger your ship retrieval. An unqualified deck draw here means cosmic deck; no reward deck is included in this scope.'),
'Reserve':('FAQ pp5,19','Choose all attacks for one use together. If the power is zapped, keep the card you were about to play. Reserve Wild need only be played once in the encounter. For the remainder of that encounter, whenever another player discards a reinforcement, its user may take that card into their own hand; this does not retrieve their own discards.'),
'Shadow':('FAQ p20','Execute for each qualifying destiny card drawn, including cards later discarded/redrawn, not only the final used card.'),
'Sorcerer':('Rulebook p14; FAQ pp12,20','Oracle forces your card faceup and selects its own before you can switch the two selected cards. Clone keeps its final card; Filch takes its opponent\'s final card. The Gambler restriction belongs to Gambler\'s sheet.'),
'Tick-Tock':('FAQ p21','If a deal gives both main players their fifth foreign colony and removes your last token, all three share the win.'),
'Trader':('FAQ p21','As defense with an empty hand, you must first draw a new hand before choosing whether to trade; you cannot trade an empty hand.'),
'Vacuum':('FAQ pp2,15,21–22','Never target yourself. Your actual warp loss triggers before Healer retrieves your ships. Under Vacuum Super, losses from a single effect occur simultaneously: Zombie cannot preserve one disappearing one-ship colony by moving its saved ship to another colony being eliminated by that same effect.'),
'Virus':('FAQ pp6,21','Virus Wild with no allies multiplies by zero. Any effect asking for a number of ships counts physical Macron ships as one apiece, not their encounter strength of four.'),
'Void':('FAQ pp18,21–22','Void eradicates directly instead of sending to warp, so it overrides Zombie and Observer; those eradicated ships earn no compensation. Void Super extends Eradicate to encounters you win as an ally. Ships protected by your minimum-five-surviving-ships limit go to warp normally instead. A failed deal is not a win and does not eradicate its three-ship penalty.'),
'Vulch':('FAQ p21','You cannot salvage the Cosmic Zap that zapped you: your use remains canceled when it is discarded. Artifacts discarded by a player using Vulch Wild against you remain eligible, potentially passing back and forth. Play Vulch Super on the first eligible flare discard; it then lasts for all eligible flare discards during the encounter, but cannot retrieve itself.'),
'Warpish':('FAQ p22','Count warp ships at your power\'s use. Ships arriving before it count; later arrivals do not retroactively increase its bonus. Use normal timing for simultaneous effects.'),
'Warrior':('Rulebook p12; FAQ p22','Accumulating experience does not require use and continues if the combat bonus is zapped. Reclaiming an empty home planet counts as a win and earns experience.'),
'Will':('FAQ pp2,22','Correct the printed timing from Launch to **Destiny**. You can choose another player\'s colony on a third player\'s planet only if that colony actually exists; you may still encounter a home-system owner on their own empty home planet. Other effects caused by the destiny card, including Shadow, still happen.'),
'Zombie':('Rulebook p12; FAQ p22','You receive no compensation for prevented warp losses; if zapped and ships really enter warp, compensation can apply. Healer cannot heal prevented losses. You may use costs such as Kamikaze Wild and prevent the ships\' arrival in warp. Void eradicates directly and overrides your prevention. Liberation as a deal term does not require use and has no numerical cap, but FAQ limits it to your negotiating opponent\'s ships, not a third player\'s. Simultaneous colony losses do not allow moving a saved ship to another colony lost in the same effect.')}

notes['Clone']=(notes['Clone'][0]+'; original rulebook p5', notes['Clone'][1]+' Clone Wild (any player, any phase) lets you retain a played artifact instead of discarding it and play it again during a later encounter, following its legal timing. Clone Super is for a main player during Resolution and requires sufficient cards in the opponent\'s hand for the chosen compensation.')
notes['Filch']=(notes['Filch'][0]+'; original rulebook p5',notes['Filch'][1]+' The classic Filch Super, as any player during Reveal, permits filching any other player\'s used encounter card whether you participated in that encounter or not.')
notes['Mind']=(notes['Mind'][0]+'; retained official May6 2009 FAQ p1',notes['Mind'][1]+' The corrected Mind Wild can be played when another player draws a cosmic-deck card. For the rest of this encounter, each time that same player must draw a card from the cosmic deck, you may draw it instead, inspect it and give it to them; choose separately for each draw and do not share that information. The one play targets that player\'s later draws.')
notes['Reincarnator']=(notes['Reincarnator'][0]+'; retained official May6 2009 FAQ p1',notes['Reincarnator'][1]+' Corrected Wild: after another player besides Reincarnator loses or fails a deal, you may give them this flare. Immediately after the encounter they discard their current power and draw a new one, rejecting Game Setup or disallowed aliens and drawing again.')

